              "text": "**Yes, Coca-Cola did originally contain cocaine.** \n\nThe history of how the drug got into the beverage, how much was actually in it, and how the recipe changed over time involves several key details:\n\n### 1. Where the Cocaine Came From\nCoca-Cola was invented in 1885 by John Stith Pemberton, an Atlanta pharmacist who was looking for a temperance-friendly alternative to his previous creation, \"French Wine Coca\". \n\nThe name **\"Coca-Cola\"** was derived directly from its two main active ingredients:\n* **\"Coca\"** represented the **coca leaf** (native to South America), which naturally contains cocaine alkaloids.\n* **\"Cola\"** represented the **kola nut**, which provided a high dose of caffeine.\n\nDuring the late 19th century, cocaine was not an illegal substance. It was highly regarded in Western medicine as a \"miracle\" drug. Pemberton marketed Coca-Cola as a patent medicine and brain tonic that could cure headaches, fatigue, and nervous exhaustion.\n\n### 2. How Much Cocaine Was in It?\nWhile early versions of the drink definitely contained cocaine, it was not an overwhelming dose. \n* Historians estimate that Pemberton's original recipe contained about **9 milligrams of cocaine per glass**. \n* For perspective, a typical modern recreational dose (a single \"line\" of cocaine) is roughly 50 to 75 milligrams. \n* Still, the 9 milligrams per glass was enough to give drinkers a noticeable, stimulate-driven \"buzz\" or energy boost.\n\n### 3. Why and When Was It Removed?\nBy the late 1890s and early 1900s, public and scientific consensus on cocaine shifted dramatically. Reports of severe addiction, cocaine-induced psychoses, and racialized panics in the American South forced the company's new owner, Asa Griggs Candler, to rethink the formula.\n\n* **1903:** Under public pressure, Candler dramatically reduced the cocaine content. The company began using \"spent\" (partially decocainized) coca leaves. This left only trace, microscopic amounts of the drug in the drink.\n* **1929:** Coca-Cola successfully transitioned to using a **completely decocainized** coca leaf extract, rendering the drink 100% cocaine-free.\n\n### 4. How Coca-Cola is Made Today\nInterestingly, Coca-Cola **still uses coca leaves** to flavor the modern soda\u2014but all of the cocaine is removed before it gets to the bottle. \n\nBy federal decree, a chemical processing plant in New Jersey operated by the **Stepan Company** is the only facility in the United States authorized to import coca leaves (primarily from Peru and Bolivia). \n1. Stepan Company imports the leaves and chemically extracts the active cocaine.\n2. The **decocainized leaf extract** is sent to Coca-Cola to be used as a secret flavoring agent.\n3. The extracted **pure cocaine** is sold to Mallinckrodt, a pharmaceutical company, which processes it into a prescription local anesthetic used by eye, ear, nose, and throat doctors. \n\n### The Company's Official Stance\nIf you look at the official Coca-Cola company website, they traditionally state that *\"cocaine has never been an added ingredient in Coca-Cola.\"* While technically true in a literal, public-relations sense\u2014meaning they never bought pure, isolated cocaine powder to dump into the syrup vat\u2014the drink absolutely contained cocaine because raw, unprocessed coca leaves containing the drug were a primary ingredient in the original recipe.",
